From 18e94308488ab28bcf9baac221fa41350a36ebe8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:59:28 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Stereo depth: KWin says what each output's wallpaper
 loses at the sides

A sunk wallpaper keeps its size, so the columns only one eye would see
are cut from both sides: (|p| + 1) / 2 per side, with p the sunk limit's
parallax on that output, lround(SunkLimit x W / 1920). The pages that set
a wallpaper must state that cost with the real number for the user's
Background depth and each screen, never a fixed 10, and never a formula
of their own.

So KWin publishes it, from the same StereoDepth code the scene cuts
with. StereoDepth::wallpaperCut(output, sunkLimit) gives an output's cut
per side from the width of its view, 0 on an output without stereo eyes.
The new D-Bus object /org/kde/KWin/StereoDepth (org.kde.KWin.StereoDepth)
has:
- WallpaperCuts (a{si}): each output, by name, with what it loses now,
  announced through PropertiesChanged when the Background depth or an
  output changes;
- WallpaperCutsAt(d): the same for a Background depth not applied yet,
  such as the one a settings page's slider shows;
- OutputsChanged: an output came or went, or its size, scale or mode
  changed, so a page asking WallpaperCutsAt asks again.

testStereoDepthWallpaperCuts checks 4 and 5 at 1280 and 1920 with the
default, 7 and 10 at 20, nothing at 0 and on a 2D output, each output's
own number side by side, and that a change of depth or mode shows at
once.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
